<Base title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
<Fragment set:html={"<section class=\"article-hero\"><div class=\"shell\"><div class=\"breadcrumb\"><a href=\"/resources/\">Resource Center</a> / <a href=\"/options-upgrades/\">Options &amp; Upgrades</a> / 12</div><div class=\"eyebrow\">CUSTOMIZATION TOPIC 12 \u00b7 Structure &amp; Exterior</div><h1>Porches, Awnings, Dormers &amp; Architectural Roofline Options</h1><p>Roofline changes affect geometry, flashing, framing and cost.</p></div><section class=\"asi-cost-review\" id=\"option-planning-prices\"><h2>Option pricing &amp; budget planning</h2><p class=\"asi-cost-alert\"><strong>Research-stage planning references:</strong> The figures below come from historical ASI planning material or approved model siding formulas. Historical amounts have NOT been verified as current Appalachian Sheds selling prices. They are not quotes. Final prices and any credits for included standard features must appear in a written proposal.</p><table class=\"asi-cost-list\"><thead><tr><th>Option / scope</th><th>Planning figure</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>New-build entry awning</td><td>$900\u2013$4,500+</td><td>installed, historical</td></tr><tr><td>New-build shed dormer</td><td>$5,000\u2013$14,000+</td><td>project, historical</td></tr><tr><td>New-build front porch</td><td>$3,500\u2013$10,500+</td><td>project, historical</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"asi-cost-note\">Standard ASI building features remain included in the approved model price. Figures shown are for planning context only; these are not added to the online shed calculator. No site-specific price should be inferred from a price per linear or square foot.</p><a href=\"/options-gallery/\">Explore visual option comparisons</a></section><section class=\"asi-option-mini-gallery\"><h2>Visual comparison \u2014 planned photographs</h2><p>Images will be added where they help explain actual differences. No placeholder is represented as an installed product or completed ASI project.</p><div class=\"asi-mini-grid\"><div class=\"asi-small-slot\">PHOTO SLOT \u2022 Overall appearance</div><div class=\"asi-small-slot\">PHOTO SLOT \u2022 Close-up detail</div><div class=\"asi-small-slot\">PHOTO SLOT \u2022 Installed scale / context</div></div></section></section><div class=\"shell deep-layout\"><article><section class=\"deep-answer\" id=\"overview\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">THE ESSENTIAL ANSWER</div><h2>What should you know before choosing?</h2><p>Choose a porch for real covered entry space. Choose an awning when the goal is doorway protection without a porch footprint. Choose a dormer for daylight, upper-wall volume and architectural character. Choose a gable overhang when the roof edge needs more depth, shadow and finish detail.</p><p>Creates useful covered entry space and a more residential arrival point. Cost depends primarily on length, 4' or 6' depth, roof structure, support/foundation, columns, decking and ceiling finish.</p></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-1\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 01</div><h2>Choose the Upgrade by the Problem You Want to Solve.</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Choose a porch for real covered entry space. Choose an awning when the goal is doorway protection without a porch footprint. Choose a dormer for daylight, upper-wall volume and architectural character. Choose a gable overhang when the roof edge needs more depth, shadow and finish detail.</p><p>Creates useful covered entry space and a more residential arrival point. Cost depends primarily on length, 4' or 6' depth, roof structure, support/foundation, columns, decking and ceiling finish.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Entry Awnings &amp; Canopies</h3><p>Protects doors and thresholds without creating a full porch. Proper backing, load path, roof slope, flashing and drainage are more important than decorative brackets alone.</p><p>Best when daylight and a broader upper-wall area matter. Width, window count, roof pitch, header / rafter changes, siding and flashing drive cost.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Gable / A-Frame Dormers</h3><p>Creates a stronger individual roof accent and works well with one feature window. Typically more framing per square foot than a simple shed dormer.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Eyebrow / Specialty Dormers</h3><p>Selected primarily for architectural character. Curved or specialty framing and flashing can make a small eyebrow dormer more labor-intensive than its size suggests.</p><p>Adds rake depth, shadow and protection at the end wall. Price depends on projection, roof width, soffit treatment, fascia / rake trim and roofing integration.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-2\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 02</div><h2>Porch Finish Package</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Columns or timber posts, beadboard or T&amp;G ceiling, decking, fascia, trim and lighting should be coordinated as one porch package rather than priced as disconnected decorative items.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Combined Architectural Package</h3><p>Porch + dormer + canopy + extended overhang combinations are priced from the elevation because framing efficiencies and conflicts depend on the final roof geometry.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>A Porch Is a Structural Addition to the Building Footprint</h3><p>Posts must bear on a suitable support system, the porch roof needs a continuous load path, and the roof must drain without concentrating water at the entry or foundation. Porch depth also changes how the building sits on the site and can affect setbacks.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Local Porch Pricing Is Broad Because Scope Varies Enormously.</h3><p>Current Cincinnati porch benchmarks range widely by size and finish. ASI's ranges below are narrowed to small detached-building porches built as part of the original project\u2014not large house porches or major remodels.</p><p>Steps, ramps and accessible approach work remain in the Ramps, Steps, Landings &amp; Building Access category so the porch is not double-priced.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-3\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 03</div><h2>Door Protection Without the Footprint of a Porch</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>An awning should extend far enough to protect the threshold and entry zone, and it should transfer roof and snow loads back into the wall framing through proper blocking or a supported post system. Flashing is essential where the canopy meets the wall.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Decorative Brackets Are Not a Load Path.</h3><p>The framing behind the siding needs to be known before a structural canopy is attached.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Dormers Change Roof Load Paths.</h3><p>Headers, rafters, sidewalls, front wall, roof sheathing, windows and flashing must be coordinated before framing.</p><p>Window pricing is coordinated with the Windows &amp; Natural Light category; dormer pricing here covers the architectural framing, roof integration, siding / trim and normal installation coordination.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-4\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 04</div><h2>More Than Decoration \u2014 But Not a Substitute for Proper Wall Water Management</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Gable overhangs improve roofline depth and help shield rake trim and upper siding from some weather exposure. Their value is architectural and protective, but they do not replace proper flashing, siding clearances or drainage.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Before Roof Framing.</h3><p>Original framing is cleaner and generally less expensive than extending an already-completed roof edge.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>These Options Can Change Footprint, Projection, Height and Structural Scope</h3><p>Within the City of Cincinnati, structural alterations and awnings are permit-review items, and accessory structures above the narrow exemption thresholds require permitting. Other Ohio, Northern Kentucky and Southeast Indiana jurisdictions use their own rules.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Confirm the Project Address Before Finalizing the Roofline Package.</h3><p>Setbacks, easements, HOA restrictions, historic-review requirements and permit thresholds can change across the Tri-State.</p><p>May change the building footprint, setback measurement, approach and foundation / support scope.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-5\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 05</div><h2>Architectural Roofline Mistakes Appalachian Sheds Should Help Customers Avoid</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>One Authoritative Location for Each Decision</h3><p>This page owns the architectural structure and pricing. Related categories retain the technical selections that belong to them.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Cross-Reference Instead of Repricing.</h3><p>That keeps the website and future calculators from drifting into contradictory prices.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"asi-scope\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">IMPORTANT PROJECT DISTINCTION</div><h2>How this relates to an Appalachian Sheds project</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Built on your property</h3><p>All Appalachian Sheds buildings are constructed on site. Property access, drainage, topography, location restrictions and the selected model can affect planning.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Exterior shell versus finished interior</h3><p>Our standard building proposal describes a weather-tight exterior shell. Insulation, electrical, plumbing, heating, cooling, and interior wall or ceiling finishes are separate work unless expressly listed in the written scope.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Only the written scope is authoritative</h3><p>Model-specific specifications, pricing, selected options, finishing requirements and any engineering or permit obligations must be confirmed for the actual building and site. Avoid treating this general guide as an engineering design or a permit determination.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"next\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">MAKE THE NEXT DECISION</div><h2>Your next planning step</h2><p>Compare your preferred roofline with the actual model design, building height, roof-material requirements and applicable local restrictions before requesting a revised written proposal.</p><div class=\"related\"><a href=\"/buildings/\">View Models &amp; Pricing</a><a href=\"/planning-guides/\">Explore Building Guides</a><a href=\"/options-upgrades/\">Browse Options &amp; Upgrades</a></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"references\"><h2>Relevant planning references</h2><p>These official and manufacturer resources provide additional background. Product and jurisdiction requirements must be checked for the exact project.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.asphaltroofing.org/installation-of-asphalt-shingles-on-lower-sloped-roofs/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Asphalt Roofing Manufacturers Association: low-slope installation</a></p></section></article><aside class=\"deep-toc\"><strong>On this page</strong><a href=\"#overview\">Essential answer</a><a href=\"#topic-1\">Choose the Upgrade by the Problem You Want to Solve.</a><a href=\"#topic-2\">Porch Finish Package</a><a href=\"#topic-3\">Door Protection Without the Footprint of a Porch</a><a href=\"#topic-4\">More Than Decoration \u2014 But Not a Substitute for Proper Wall Water Management</a><a href=\"#topic-5\">Architectural Roofline Mistakes Appalachian Sheds Should Help Customers Avoid</a><a href=\"#asi-scope\">How this relates to an ASI project</a><a href=\"#references\">Planning references</a><a class=\"btn light\" href=\"/buildings/\">See Your Price</a></aside></div>"} />
</Base>

